virglrenderer: Venus dedicated memory goes before its image or buffer

What: vkr links memory made for one image or buffer (VkMemoryDedicatedAllocateInfo)
to that object. Only that object binds to the memory, at offset 0: another image or
a buffer there is refused (the bind ends the app's context). On KosmicKrisp alone,
ALIAS_BIT images may bind to memory dedicated to an ALIAS_BIT image, at offset 0: guest
Mesa binds a swapchain's memory that way (VkBindImageMemorySwapchainInfoKHR). A dedicated
struct chained twice, or a target of another device, is refused at the allocation. When
the app destroys the image or buffer while such memory lives, vkr drops its own object at
once and keeps the driver's until the last of that memory is freed: memory first, then
the object, also when the context ends. The links sit under a device mutex that is never
held across a driver call.
Why: MoltenVK keeps the dedicated image or buffer in the memory and writes to it
when the memory is freed (~MVKDeviceMemory). An image destroyed before it was bound
(legal) left a dangling pointer there, written after its free (seen under
libgmalloc). MoltenVK also took an ALIAS_BIT image on memory dedicated to another
image without counting it, and locked the freed memory's mutex when that image was
destroyed later, so that bind stays refused on MoltenVK, also for a swapchain's image.
KosmicKrisp ignores dedicated allocations. vkr reads the first struct of a type and
MoltenVK's loop keeps the last, so a second dedicated struct got past the link.
Upstream: OmacVM policy (VUIDs vkBindImageMemory-memory-02628 and -10926,
vkBindBufferMemory-memory-01508 and -10925, VkMemoryAllocateInfo-sType-unique; no Mac
driver has dedicatedAllocationImageAliasing, the exception of 02629); MoltenVK issue
draft, not posted.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-commands.c (the x- cases, also under libgmalloc).

